  "summary": "The board left the rate unchanged and said it expects inflation to reach its target in the fourth quarter of 2027. The headline rate is close to the Mexican central bank's target, but underlying inflation remained more persistent. The lawmakers asked the US trade representative to prioritize measurable changes that strengthen trade and competitiveness.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "Mexico's central bank holds its policy rate at 6.50 percent",
    "context": "The board left the rate unchanged and said it expects inflation to reach its target in the fourth quarter of 2027.",
    "background": "Mexico's central bank uses the overnight policy rate to influence borrowing and spending. A rate above inflation can slow prices, but it also makes credit more expensive.",
    "view": "July's inflation report explains the wait. Headline inflation is close to target, but underlying inflation is falling more slowly. Mexico's central bank is waiting for that underlying measure to improve before making credit cheaper again.",
    "prediction": "The next tests will be the September 9 inflation release and the September 24 rate decision. If underlying inflation falls again, a cut becomes more likely. If it reverses, another hold becomes more likely.",
