      "headline": "US lawmakers call for a tougher review of the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement",
      "context": "The lawmakers asked the US trade representative to prioritize measurable changes that strengthen trade and competitiveness.",
      "background": "The agreement remains in force without a new extension. Because the three countries did not agree to a 16-year extension in July, they will return to the review every year and the treaty can continue through 2036.",
      "view": "The letter raises negotiating pressure, but it does not change tariffs or treaty rules. Existing factories keep their current access. The practical cost is uncertainty for companies considering investments that take years to repay.",
      "prediction": "The demands are likely to matter only if they appear in formal US negotiating objectives or draft text. Specific Mexican commitments on market access or outside-country investment would confirm that the letter is shaping the review.",
